From lawrence-hunsicker at uiowa.edu  Thu Aug  6 04:07:48 2009
From: lawrence-hunsicker at uiowa.edu (Hunsicker, Lawrence)
Date: Wed, 05 Aug 2009 23:07:48 -0500
Subject: Testing significance of differences in nested robust survival models
Message-ID: <2B80F69A8A189D48B0E668B0BBC6BA4201E01344@HC-MAIL13.healthcare.uiowa.edu>

Hi, folks:  

Two related questions today:

I am running a set of survival analyses with a time dependent "era"
variable.  Some cases span the two eras, so I have created different
records for the pieces of one patient's record in the two eras.  I have
used the coxph Surv(start,stop,censor) version of coxph, and included a
"cluster(pt.id)" term to account for the fact that the two pieces
represent the same individual.  I have several nested models, and I want
to test for significance of differences in fit between the nested
models.

The two questions:

a)	The likelihood ratio test evidently does not take into account
the clustering.  One is told to use the Wald or robust score tests,
which evidently use the robust variance estimates, taking the clustering
into account.  The coxph program gives a Wald test and a p value for
comparing the submitted model with a null model.  But I can't find an
"anova" like function to compare two nested models when neither is the
null model.  My instinct is to compute (Wald score 1-Wald score 2) and
estimate a p-value from the chi-square distribution with (df1-df2)
degrees of freedom - as one would do for a likelihood ratio test.  But
my mathematical statistics is not up to being sure that this is correct.
Is this the correct calculation?  Is there an existing "anova" type
function that does this for a robust survival model?

b)	I have a vague recollection that since by definition in a "time
to first event" model, the first piece of a patient's observation can't
ever have an event if there is a second piece, one actually doesn't need
to put in the cluster term - that it is only correlation of events, not
of exposure, that requires use of the cluster term.  Is this correct?
If it is, I could just drop the cluster term and use the likelihood
ratio test.  (I have written my own one of these since I couldn't find
an S-function to do it.  But there must be such an S function.  Any
suggestions for that, while I am at it?)

As always, my thanks in advance to any of you that can help me with the
above.

Larry Hunsicker

