Subject: Least Trimmed Squares Query
Message-ID: <4A94166E.4050906@noaa.gov>

I'm using a least trimmed squares routine and am trying to pass along 
examples of the process.

I chose the following data set out of thin air:

x: 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9
y: 10, 12, 14, 16, 20, 22, 24, 28, 30

I have 9 data points and two parameters, so as a first attempt I'll 
choose all subsamples of 6 data points (floor(n+p+1)/2). When I use lqs 
(out of MASS) with psamp = 6 and nsamp = "exact," I get an intercept of 
7.23 and a slope of 2.51.

If I then simply fit an lm to all of the possible 84 combinations of 9 
choose 6 indices, I get the following data points that yield the 
smallest sum of squares:

x: 1, 2, 3, 6, 8, 9
y: 10, 12, 14, 22, 28, 30

Using lm to fit these, I get an intercept of 6.88 and a slope of 2.58.

I must misunderstand something, because my question is: why aren't these 
the same?

Kim Elmore

