> example, I need a formula that looks like, say:
> 
> observation ~ R + Z + uvel
> 
> where R is the name of column 4, Z is the name of column 5, 
> and uvel is 
> the name of column 7 in my 9 column data frame.
> 
> I'd like to construct this formula from the vector c(4,5,7). 
> So, when I 
> hand my formula-generating function c(4,5,7) it generates a formula 
> class object that is observation ~ R + Z + uvel.

One way is to use paste() to create strings containing the formulae.
E.g.,
   > predictorNames <- names(fuel.frame)[c(1,2,5)]
   > formulae <- apply(combinations(length(predictorNames), 2), 2, 
               function(which)paste("Mileage ~",
paste(predictorNames[which], collapse=" + ")))
   > formulae
   [1] "Mileage ~ Weight + Disp."
   [2] "Mileage ~ Weight + Type" 
   [3] "Mileage ~ Disp. + Type" 
   > lm(formulae[2], data=fuel.frame)
   Call:
   lm(formula = formulae[2], data = fuel.frame)

   Coefficients:
    (Intercept)       Weight TypeLarge TypeMedium TypeSmall TypeSporty
TypeVan 
        40.2053 -0.005697258  1.074954 -0.2289456  3.657357   1.740694
-1.310085

   Degrees of freedom: 60 total; 53 residual
   Residual standard error: 2.39094 

Most modelling functions will parse a string given as the formula
argument.  If not parse it with parse(text=string) and hand the result
to the function.  If you want the call in the output to look right you
can use some trickery with do.call():

   > do.call("lm", list(formula=formulae[2],
data=as.name("fuel.frame")))
   Call:
   lm(formula = "Mileage ~ Weight + Type", data = fuel.frame)
   
   Coefficients:
    (Intercept)       Weight TypeLarge TypeMedium TypeSmall TypeSporty
TypeVan 
