From Harry.Southworth at astrazeneca.com  Fri Feb 19 13:45:32 2010
From: Harry.Southworth at astrazeneca.com (Southworth, Harry)
Date: Fri, 19 Feb 2010 13:45:32 -0000
Subject: Safe prediction with coxph
In-Reply-To: <1252537769.17589.9.camel@punchbuggy>
References: <2B80F69A8A189D48B0E668B0BBC6BA4203E76ABB@HC-MAIL13.healthcare.uiowa.edu> <1252537769.17589.9.camel@punchbuggy>
Message-ID: <5FEC4F8459B55448AB1D8E8D37D2B238058D51E7@ukaprdembx02.rd.astrazeneca.net>

S+ 7.0.6 or 8.1.1 on Windows.

I'm using coxph with pspline, something like this:

mod <- coxph(Surv(d, s) ~ pspline(x, df=4) + trt, data=mydata)

I want to plot the predicted risk across the range of x, something like
this:

myx <- seq(min(x), max(x), len=100)
p <- predict(mod, newdata=data.frame(x=myx, trt=rep("A", 100)),
type="risk")
plot(x, p, type = "l")

Being concerned about safe prediction, I looked into what might be a
good way of doing this and found that in S+, predict.coxph has an
undocumented argument, safe = F:

args(predict.coxph)
function(object, newdata = NULL, type = c("lp", "risk", "expected",
"terms"),
	se.fit = F, terms = labels.lm(object), collapse, safe = F, ...)


Searching around, I found some references to this argument in some R
documentation, though the argument is not available to predict.coxph in
my R 2.10.1 installation. What I found didn't give much detail.

I'd be grateful if anyone can point out to me where I can find more
information on safe prediction in Cox models with S+. It seems obvious
that I should specify safe = TRUE in the call to predict.coxph, but I'm
not altogether comfortable blindly using undocumented functionality.

Thanks,
Harry

