Message-ID: <B37C0A15B8FB3C468B5BC7EBC7DA14CC63352578A9@LP-EXMBVS10.CO.IHC.COM>

There are some attempts at GUI's for R, whether they are nice or not is somewhat in the eye of the beholder.

The ones that come to mind (could be others that I am not familiar with):

Rcmdr, I like this one for intro classes, you can do standard graphs and analyses by clicking on menus and filling in dialog boxes.  There is a mechanism to add your own menus if the original ones don't include something that you want (the first example of this includes demonstrations from the TeachingDemos package, that may bias me in its favor).  It also shows the command line equivalent command for what you just did, so that students can move from GUI to command line if they want to.

RExcel, this uses MS Excel as the front end with R as the computational engine.  Good for students/departments who only want to use excel.  It includes the menu bar from Rcmdr so you get the same menu/dialog interface plus doing many things by right clicking on a selection in the spreadsheet.

R-Plus by xlsolutions corp., they have a free version as well as a commercial version (more bells and whistles).  See http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1qsv1MdB4tk for a short demo.

JGR, a java based GUI for R.

-- 
Gregory (Greg) L. Snow Ph.D.
Statistical Data Center
Intermountain Healthcare
