This post by Gérald Jean prompts me to share my concerns with the future of
S+.

I have been using S+ for ten years now and consider myself reasonably
proficient coding in S. However the datasets I analyse are becoming ever
larger and I find myself increasingly performing work arounds to analyse
the data (splitting operations in script files to do intermediate
calculations to reduce the size of the dataset) . I don't believe the Big
Data library will help me with the operations I carry out (more data
manipulation than statistical analysis). Does the Big Data library help to
perform a tapply on a huge dataset? My concern is that the data sets will
get so large that I will have to abandon S+.

I am constrained to the Windows environment by my companies IT policy and I
have never got a clear answer whether a Unix/Linux environment would be
more effective. On Windows XP increasing my RAM doesn't seem to work - S+
doesn't access it. I have colleagues in other firms that use Matlab and
though I never done an exact comparison it seems to perform better with
very large datasets. Matlab also has a true 64 bit version which in theory
raiss the ceiling on datasets remarkably.

I have noticed over the years that concerns about memory usage and large
datasets crops up from time to time but I've never seen a convincing post
that reassures. Should I be taking a closer look at Matlab?

Any thoughts?

Regards,
Glenn Treacy

