Glenn Treacy said:

> I am constrained to the Windows environment by my company's IT policy and > I have never got a clear answer whether a Unix/Linux environment would be > more effective. On Windows XP increasing my RAM doesn't seem to work - S+ > doesn't access it. I have colleagues in other firms that use Matlab and 
> though I never done an exact comparison it seems to perform better with 
> very large datasets. Matlab also has a true 64 bit version which in theory > raise the ceiling on datasets remarkably.

I do my data management in SAS and then bring the final analysis sets into S-Plus for statistical analysis and graphics.  SAS has a 64 bit Windows version.  But the question above is one that should be addressed by TIBCO.  Windows XP is only a 32 bit OS, but Windows 7 does have a 64 bit system.  S-Plus has not yet (to my knowledge) developed a 64 bit application. What is TIBCO's plan with respect to this?  Will there be a 64 bit version of S-Plus?  That would help in a major way with analysis of large data sets.  I am not naïve about the effort and time involved in developing and deploying a 64 bit version of an application.  But S-Plus will not be viable in the longer haul if it does not develop a 64 bit version.  

Larry Hunsicker



-----Original Message-----
From: s-news-owner at lists.biostat.wustl.edu [mailto:s-news-owner at lists.biostat.wustl.edu] On Behalf Of Treacy, Glenn
Sent: Wednesday, April 28, 2010 12:58 PM
To: s-news at lists.biostat.wustl.edu
Subject: Re: [S] Future of S-Plus???

This post by Gérald Jean prompts me to share my concerns with the future of S+. 

I have been using S+ for ten years now and consider myself reasonably proficient coding in S. However the datasets I analyse are becoming ever larger and I find myself increasingly performing work arounds to analyse the data (splitting operations in script files to do intermediate calculations to reduce the size of the dataset) . I don't believe the Big Data library will help me with the operations I carry out (more data manipulation than statistical analysis). Does the Big Data library help to perform a tapply on a huge dataset? My concern is that the data sets will get so large that I will have to abandon S+.

I am constrained to the Windows environment by my companies IT policy and I have never got a clear answer whether a Unix/Linux environment would be more effective. On Windows XP increasing my RAM doesn't seem to work - S+ doesn't access it. I have colleagues in other firms that use Matlab and though I never done an exact comparison it seems to perform better with very large datasets. Matlab also has a true 64 bit version which in theory raiss the ceiling on datasets remarkably.

I have noticed over the years that concerns about memory usage and large datasets crops up from time to time but I've never seen a convincing post that reassures. Should I be taking a closer look at Matlab?

Any thoughts?

Regards,
Glenn Treacy

