Gerald Jean wrote:
"But my main reason in favor of Splus is the way it manages its workspaces.  In R when you attach a workspace R will load in memory the full workspace with all its objects.  This is fine for people working on small projects and / or small data sets.  When you get into multiple data sets, multi Giga bytes each, it is silly, in my opinion, to load all that in memory when you might use only one or two in a given session.  Further more, this approach is very slow at startup and at shut down and it eats up a whole chunk of the available memory, leaving a much reduced memory space for the users.  Splus loads objects in memory upon request, i.e. only when needed, hence, the workspaces could be huge but you'll pay the price, time and memory wise, only for the requested objects."

The “g.data” package in R lets you manage your workspaces closer to the S-Plus paradigm.  Objects are stored on disk individually, and while they are all visible to R immediately, they are only actually loaded into memory as needed.  Also, they are only saved back to disk at the user’s request, so disk I/O is minimized.  “g.data” is available on CRAN.

-- David Brahm (brahm at alum.mit.edu) 
