[1] 99  ## implying 98 different missingness patterns, the first pattern
being the non-missing observations.

hence a reduction in time of more than 50%, thanks Bill! and a little
reduction on memory too.  Another avantage of Bill's solution is that the
output list has, as names of the list, the missing patterns themselves.

Let me explain what I will do with that.  The data I am working with for
this part of my project is socio-demographic data.  Most variables have
missing values and I would like to impute those missing values by averaging
over their k nearest neighbors.  Splus has the spatialstats library with
the "quad.tree" and "find.neigbor" functions, both amazingly efficients.
Each observation in my socio-demo data set represents a unique postal code
and I do have the spatial coordinates of all those postal codes.

Once I have the missingness patterns, for each unique pattern I will find
the k nearest neighbors, based on all other observations, and impute the
pattern's missing variables by averaging (weighted or not) over those k
nearest neighbors.

Although I know pretty much how I am going to implement this, any
suggestions on efficiency would be welcome,

Thanks again to Bill Dunlap and also to David Lorenz, who first noticed I
had the data.frames in the wrong order in "match.data.frame".

