Walt,
  That is relatively easy if you expand the dimnames using expand.grid. 
Here's your example with an additional NA value to show that it works for 
more than the first value.
The matrix, called Mat:
     A B  C  D 
  L NA 4  7 10
JAR  2 5 NA 11
 TM  3 6  9 12

The call to extract the row and column names:
expand.grid(dimnames(Mat))[is.na(Mat),]

The output:
  Var1 Var2 
1    L    A
8  JAR    C

  All you need to know is that Var1 is the rown ame and Var2 is the cclumn 
name.
Dave



