I believe this is why the default output format for
timeSpan objects uses days (+ hours, etc.).
The help file for class.timeSpan does say that
the %y and %D formats use 365-day "years".
 
Since the timeSpan object contains only the
difference between two dates, not the start and
finish date, it doesn't have the information to
do better than that.
 
Look at the timeRelative class if you need to
look at time differences in terms of variable
length things like years and months or even
the number of business days (with your choice
of holidays).

Bill Dunlap
