From lorenz at usgs.gov  Thu Nov 18 14:56:16 2010
From: lorenz at usgs.gov (David L Lorenz)
Date: Thu, 18 Nov 2010 08:56:16 -0600
Subject: Re: Event counts within time spans
In-Reply-To: <4CE45C2F.9010508@noaa.gov>
References: <4CE45C2F.9010508@noaa.gov>
Message-ID: <OF4083829E.3FD16FF6-ON862577DF.0050308E-862577DF.00520E9C@usgs.gov>

Kim,
  I'm guessing that you have a data set with at least 2 columns one for 
the time of the event (timeDate), and one for the value. The function 
aggregateSeries can be used to produce a summary that you described.  For 
the example below, I have a data set called MD, with columns DT (time 
Date), and EV. The call to produce a count of event in each month is

with(MD, aggregateSeries(timeSeries(EV, DTt), FUN=length, 
pos=timeSeq('10/01/2002', '12/31/2005', by='months'), drop.empty=F))

  I'll leave it up to you to read the documentation to figure out what 
does what and to figure out how to create a 15-minute time sequence.
Dave



